#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100670 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "4d942842-ec76-5ea5-9591-e8b8b8732425" :question/math-academy-id "e-6605" :question/problem "Find the specific solution of the first-order ODE\n$xe^y\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}+e^y = e^{x} \\,$,\ngiven that $y(1)=1$ and $x>0$." :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n$z=e^y\\,$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=e^y\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}+z=e^x\\,\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(1)=e^{y(1)}=e^1=e\\,\n$$\n\nNotice that the left-hand side of the differential equation is now an exact derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx\\frac{dz}{dx} + z &= \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(xz) &= e^{x} \\\\\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(xz)dx &= ∫e^{x}dx \\\\\nxz &= e^{x} + C \\\\\nz &= \\frac{e^{x} + C}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(1)=e$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz(1) &= e \\\\\n\\frac{e^{1} + C}{1} &= e \\\\\ne + C &= e \\\\\nC &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{e^x}{x}\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=e^y$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ne^{y} &= z \\\\\ne^{y} &= \\frac{e^{x}}{x} \\\\\ny &= \\ln (\\frac{e^{x}}{x}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (e^{x}) - \\ln (x) \\\\\n&= x - \\ln (x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0$. So, the final solution is\n\n$$\ny = x - \\ln (x), \\quad x > 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100671 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "53e6ba68-a2bc-5761-b32f-4b01057ea925" :question/math-academy-id "e-6606" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following first-order ODE using the substitution $z=y^3$.\n$xy^2\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = x^2+y^3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's make a substitution\n$z=y^3\\,$.\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}=3y^2\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\\right)=x^2+z\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{3}{x}(x^{2} + z) \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= 3x + \\frac{3}{x}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z &= 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x) =-\\frac{3}{x}, Q(x) = 3x\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(x) &= e^{∫P(x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{∫(-3/x)dx} \\\\\n&= e^{-3\\ln x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^3}\n$$\n\nyields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}}(\\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x}z) &= \\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot 3x \\\\\n\\frac{1}{x^{3}} \\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx} - \\frac{3}{x^{4}}z &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}} \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}}) &= \\frac{3}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{z}{x^{3}})dx &= ∫\\frac{3}{x^{2}}dx \\\\\n\\frac{z}{x^{3}} &= -\\frac{3}{x} + C \\\\\nz &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z=y^3$. This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{3} &= z \\\\\ny^{3} &= -3x^{2} + Cx^{3} \\\\\ny &= \\sqrt[3]{-3x^{2} + Cx^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100676 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5238ea-c44b-54dd-829b-0da094fb43e6" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a First-Order Linear ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "c906c74a-0478-5b2f-9340-b9bb7c1d2dfd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6867" :question/problem "The substitution $z= y^2$ can be used to transform the equation\n$\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} = \\dfrac{x+2}{2y} + 2x^2y$\ninto an equation of the form $z'(x) + P(x)z(x) = Q(x)$. What is the function $P(x)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n$z= y^{2}$.\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{2z^{1/2}}\\frac{dz}{dx} &= \\frac{x + 2}{2z^{1/2}} + 2x^{2}z^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dz}{dx} &= x + 2 + 4x^{2}z \\\\\n\\frac{dz}{dx} - 4x^{2}z &= x + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x) =-4x^2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd3d59c-34e0-571f-95f2-3d05c4854017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x^{2}}{C - x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098745} {:answer/id #uuid "41bbf951-aa26-52c9-8709-c206091beb9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x}{C - 2x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098746} {:answer/id #uuid "4a875131-a5ef-555c-88fe-10c3cbba4761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x^{2}}{C - 3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098747} {:answer/id #uuid "69e6fa6a-384a-5822-b0a4-50fc85d898f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x}{C - x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098748} {:answer/id #uuid "75b3114b-d9d2-5df8-bc13-d0be71f33055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x}{C - 3x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41bbf951-aa26-52c9-8709-c206091beb9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x}{C - 2x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098746} :answer-field/id #uuid "80134138-d064-5cfb-9406-b08905da5331" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098744}] :question/id #uuid "ffe3c5eb-c32b-557c-96a7-7ea03bab3fa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17544" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y + 2x^{2}y^{2}}{x}$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa4d21a1-ef78-5890-83af-7e18fb3054c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt{\\frac{x + C}{1 + x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098757} {:answer/id #uuid "02f7f62c-3b06-5625-abd7-1e3b4f188b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt{\\frac{2x + C}{2 + x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098758} {:answer/id #uuid "23777ea0-068f-55dd-ab0c-987cb660046c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x + C}{1 + x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098759} {:answer/id #uuid "2438c0a7-0a9d-5e34-b3c8-b86e32c1a4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{2x}{C - x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098760} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa8e95e-c21a-5368-bba3-da00fa9ce488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt{\\frac{x + C}{1 + x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5aa8e95e-c21a-5368-bba3-da00fa9ce488" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\sqrt{\\frac{x + C}{1 + x^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098761} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0e4d87-909b-5582-9b05-21295bb93db6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098756}] :question/id #uuid "2d210aec-e526-5356-8a8f-2d0e99d8b980" :question/math-academy-id "q-17546" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n$2(1 + x^{2})\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{y} - 2xy, y > 0$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z = y^{2}$.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "910c3b29-8411-5178-aa08-da5973f17ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x}{x + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098775} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcb3f1d-f9fb-5dc7-b71d-39c214a17018" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x + 4}{4x - 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098776} {:answer/id #uuid "bc723036-5cf4-5a5a-9486-3aeacd5767d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3x + 7}{1 + x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098777} {:answer/id #uuid "e9fde202-15df-576e-82af-03b07d36e9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5x}{5x - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098778} {:answer/id #uuid "24f873ae-89d3-51b6-9a73-a9e200f4f395" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{x - 6}{x - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9fde202-15df-576e-82af-03b07d36e9c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{5x}{5x - 4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098778} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d6510d-8ad6-560a-9d54-6c59a126adf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098774}] :question/id #uuid "1ccfb013-777e-5e8a-8a81-f7a1a7ca50df" :question/math-academy-id "q-17559" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + (y(y - 1))/(x) = 0, y(1) = 5, x > 0$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + \\frac{1}{x}z = \\frac{1}{x}$\nusing the substitution $z = y^{-1}$.*"}]}
